eukostasis Syndrome: compelling due to hyperleukocytosis with blasts and neurological symptoms, but definitive diagnosis requires confirmation of blast morphology and clinical correlation; currently at high suspicion but not yet confirmed", "Infectious Mononucleosis-like Illness: less likely given age, absence of lymphadenopathy or pharyngitis, and presence of blasts", "Metabolic Encephalopathy: possible contributor due to systemic illness, but normal electrolytes, glucose, and renal function reduce likelihood as primary cause", "Diabetic Autonomic Neuropathy: cannot explain acute visual, constitutional, or haematological findings", "Primary CNS Neoplasm: possible given neurological symptoms, but lacks focal deficits or increased intracranial pressure signs; less likely without contrast imaging"], "alarm_features": ["Night sweats", "Unintentional weight loss (10 lbs)", "Fever (37.8°C)", "Marked leukocytosis with 35% blasts", "Multisystem involvement (constitutional, neurological, haematological)"]}, "branches": {"B1": {"id": "B1", "label": "Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "expanded", "prior": 0.0, "posterior": 0.0, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The subacute presentation with 35% blasts is inconsistent with chronic-phase chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms but does not exclude blast crisis, which fall…"], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": ["B1.1", "B1.2", "B1.3"], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.9, "interrupt_relevance": 0.95, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B2": {"id": "B2", "label": "Lymphoid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.15445544554455445, "posterior": 0.2603857566765579, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": ["While a 35% blast count could technically be consistent with ALL, the patient's age (57 years) and lack of lymphoid-specific features make ALL less likely, pro…"], "evidence_against": ["The absence of lymphadenopathy, skin lesions, or other lymphoid-specific features reduces the likelihood of a de novo lymphoid neoplasm like ALL, weakening sup…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 2.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.85, "interrupt_relevance": 0.9, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B3": {"id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "parent": "ROOT", "level": 1, "status": "live", "prior": 0.20594059405940593, "posterior": 0.09643916913946586, "danger": 0.75, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.5, "evidence_for": ["The subacute presentation with 35% blasts could represent blast crisis of a chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm, which is a sub-entity within the chronic myelo…"], "evidence_against": ["The subacute presentation with 35% blasts is inconsistent with chronic-phase chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms but does not exclude blast crisis, which fall…"], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.5, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "family", "classification_axis": "mechanism", "representative_diseases": []}, "B4": {"id": "B4", "label": "Lymphoproliferative / Plasma Cell Disorder", "level": 1, "status": "parked", "posterior": 0.074, "danger": 0.6, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B5": {"id": "B5", "label": "Reactive / Non-malignant Leukocytosis", "level": 1, "status": "closed_for_now", "posterior": 0.048, "danger": 0.3, "parent": "ROOT", "closure_reason": "", "evidence_against": []}, "B1.1": {"id": "B1.1", "label": "De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.2740902701858504, "posterior": 0.2740902701858504, "danger": 0.85, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.7, "interrupt_relevance": 0.85, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.2": {"id": "B1.2", "label": "CML Blast Crisis", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.16445416211151023, "posterior": 0.16445416211151023, "danger": 0.9, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.9, "interrupt_relevance": 0.95, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}, "B1.3": {"id": "B1.3", "label": "MDS with Excess Blasts / Secondary AML", "parent": "B1", "level": 2, "status": "live", "prior": 0.08222708105575512, "posterior": 0.08222708105575512, "danger": 0.8, "actionability": 0.0, "explanatory_coverage": 0.0, "expand_score": 0.0, "evidence_for": [], "evidence_against": [], "unresolved_questions": [], "children": [], "closure_reason": "", "turn_cost_to_refine": 1.0, "diagnosis_commitment_gain": 0.75, "interrupt_relevance": 0.8, "level_role": "disease_class", "classification_axis": "etiology", "representative_diseases": []}}, "frontier": ["B1", "B2", "B3"], "other_mass": 0.0, "pending_results": [], "actions_taken": [{"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of 35% blasts in the context of a subacute presentation with constitutional symptoms and neurological dysfunction strongly support a diagnosis of acute myeloid leukemia as a myeloid…", "summary": "A 35% blast count meets diagnostic criteria for acute leukemia and, in the context of systemic symptoms and neurological dysfunction, strongly supports a myeloid neoplasm with increased blasts, such as AML."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Could the 35% blast count in a 57-year-old be consistent with acute lymphoblastic leukemia, particularly if myeloid markers are absent?", "summary": "While a 35% blast count could technically be consistent with ALL, the patient's age (57 years) and lack of lymphoid-specific features make ALL less likely, providing only moderate support for lymphoid neoplasm with increased blasts."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Is there any evidence that the leukocytosis with blasts could represent a blast crisis of a chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm rather than de novo acute leukemia?", "summary": "The subacute presentation with 35% blasts could represent blast crisis of a chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm, which is a sub-entity within the chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm family; this prevents strong exclusion and supports reopening consideration of blast-phase CML."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of lymphoid-specific markers or clinical features (e.g., lymphadenopathy, skin involvement) weaken the case for lymphoid neoplasm with increased blasts despite the presence of blasts?", "summary": "The absence of lymphadenopathy, skin lesions, or other lymphoid-specific features reduces the likelihood of a de novo lymphoid neoplasm like ALL, weakening support for B2."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the subacute onset with 35% blasts argue against a chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm, which typically presents with lower blast counts and more gradual progression?", "summary": "The subacute presentation with 35% blasts is inconsistent with chronic-phase chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms but does not exclude blast crisis, which falls under the same branch family."}, {"t": 1, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "What evidence is MOST INCONSISTENT with Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts? Identify the single strongest finding that argues against this diagnosis.", "summary": "No finding is strongly inconsistent with Myeloid Neoplasm with Increased Blasts; anemia, thrombocytopenia, leukocytosis, and blasts are all expected. The lack of specific myeloid markers is not reported and does not contradict the diagnosis."}], "differential_history": [], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "The patient has a markedly elevated white blood cell count with 35% blasts, indicating a high likelihood of acute leukemia. The presence of neurologic symptoms (headache, blurry vision, ataxia) in the context of leukocytosis raises concern for leukostasis, a life-threatening complication of hyperleukocytosis that can lead to cerebral hyperviscosity and hemorrhage. This constitutes a time-critical oncologic emergency requiring immediate intervention before diagnostic refinement.", "required_actions": ["Initiate emergency leukapheresis consult or hematologist emergent evaluation", "Obtain urgent peripheral blood smear review for blast percentage and schistocytes", "Assess for other signs of leukostasis (e.g., respiratory symptoms, altered mental status)", "Avoid blood transfusions that may increase viscosity without addressing blast count", "Initiate hydration cautiously, avoiding volume overload", "Prepare for potential emergent chemotherapy or cytoreduction"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": ""}, "turn_budget_used": 0, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.85, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.0, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Acute myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Chronic lymphocytic leukemia"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Chronic myelogenous leukemia"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Multiple myeloma"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Multiple myeloma'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B1.1", "label": "De Novo Acute Myeloid Leukemia", "posterior": 0.274, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 0, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 1, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2", "B1.2", "B3"]}}, "parent_branch": {"id": "B3", "label": "Chronic Myeloproliferative Neoplasm", "level": 1, "posterior": 0.09643916913946586, "danger": 0.75, "evidence_for": ["The subacute presentation with 35% blasts could represent blast crisis of a chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm, which is a sub-entity within the chronic myeloproliferative neoplasm family; this prevents strong exclusion and supports reopening consideration of blast-phase CML."], "evidence_against": ["A 35% blast count meets diagnostic criteria for acute leukemia and, in the context of systemic symptoms and neurological dysfunction, strongly supports a myeloid neoplasm with increased blasts, such as AML.", "The subacute presentation with 35% blasts is inconsistent with chronic-phase chronic myeloproliferative neoplasms but does not exclude blast crisis, which falls under the same branch family."], "unresolved_questions": [], "askable_discriminators": ["Known history of elevated WBC or platelets?", "Any prior splenomegaly or thrombotic events?"], "requestable_discriminators": ["BCR-A