tent": "Could polycythemia vera be in a spent phase with secondary anemia, thus explaining the current cytopenias and leukocytosis with blasts?", "summary": "PV in a 'spent phase' can present with cytopenias and leukoerythroblastosis, but the high blast count (35%) is atypical and suggests transformation to AML rather than spent PV. This makes PV unlikely as a primary explanation."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of reported basophilia, prior history of leukocytosis, or splenomegaly make CML blast crisis less likely compared to de novo AML in this 57-year-old patient?", "summary": "The lack of basophilia, prior leukocytosis, or splenomegaly reduces the likelihood of CML blast crisis, making de novo AML more probable. However, CML can remain occult until blast phase, so it is not ruled out."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the lack of documented prior cytopenias or dysplastic blood counts argue against MDS with excess blasts evolving to secondary AML in this patient?", "summary": "The absence of prior cytopenias or dysplasia reduces the likelihood of antecedent MDS, making secondary AML less likely than de novo AML. However, undiagnosed early MDS remains possible."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the absence of lymphadenopathy, hepatosplenomegaly, or lymphoid markers in a 57-year-old make acute lymphoblastic leukemia highly unlikely despite the presence of 35% blasts?", "summary": "ALL is less common in older adults and typically presents with lymphoid organomegaly. The absence of these features makes ALL unlikely, though not impossible without immunophenotyping."}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the presence of 35% blasts rule out chronic myeloid leukemia in chronic phase, which is defined by less than 10% blasts in blood or bone marrow?", "summary": "Chronic-phase CML is defined by <10% blasts; this patient has 35%, which definitively excludes chronic-phase CM